@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Stream API in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <!-- HEADER -->
        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Stream API in Java</h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn the Java Stream API for processing collections and data
                using filtering, mapping, sorting, reducing, grouping,
                matching, and collecting operations.
            </p>
        </div>


        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What is Stream API?</h3>

            <p>
                The Stream API provides a functional-style approach for
                processing sequences of data.
            </p>

            <p>
                A stream does not store data. Instead, it represents a
                pipeline of operations performed on a data source such as a
                collection, array, or generated sequence.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Concept</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                                     Data Source
                                         ↓
                                       Stream
                                         ↓
                                Intermediate Operations
                                         ↓
                                  Terminal Operation
                                         ↓
                                       Result</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- BASIC STREAM -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Creating a Stream</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of(
        "Amit",
        "Neha",
        "Rahul"
    );

names.stream()
    .forEach(
        name -&gt;
            System.out.println(name)
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- STREAM PIPELINE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Stream Pipeline</h3>

            <p>
                A stream pipeline normally contains a source, zero or more
                intermediate operations, and one terminal operation.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>numbers.stream()
    .filter(number -&gt; number &gt; 10)
    .map(number -&gt; number * 2)
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>

            <ul class="mt-3">
                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>stream()</code> creates the stream.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>filter()</code> selects elements.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <code>map()</code> transforms elements.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <code>forEach()</code> consumes the result.
                </li>
            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERMEDIATE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Intermediate Operations</h3>

            <p>
                Intermediate operations transform or refine a stream and
                return another stream.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Operation</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>filter()</code></td>
                            <td>Selects elements matching a condition.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>map()</code></td>
                            <td>Transforms each element.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>flatMap()</code></td>
                            <td>Flattens nested stream structures.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>distinct()</code></td>
                            <td>Removes duplicate elements.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>sorted()</code></td>
                            <td>Sorts elements.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>limit()</code></td>
                            <td>Restricts the number of elements.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>skip()</code></td>
                            <td>Skips the first elements.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>peek()</code></td>
                            <td>Allows an action for debugging/observation.</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TERMINAL -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Terminal Operations</h3>

            <p>
                Terminal operations produce a result or side effect and
                consume the stream.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Operation</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>forEach()</code></td>
                            <td>Performs an action for each element.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>collect()</code></td>
                            <td>Collects results into a data structure.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>reduce()</code></td>
                            <td>Combines elements into a single result.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>count()</code></td>
                            <td>Counts elements.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>min()</code></td>
                            <td>Finds the minimum element.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>max()</code></td>
                            <td>Finds the maximum element.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>findFirst()</code></td>
                            <td>Returns the first element if present.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>anyMatch()</code></td>
                            <td>Checks whether any element matches.</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- FILTER -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">filter()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>filter()</code> operation keeps elements that satisfy
                a condition.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        10,
        15,
        20,
        25,
        30
    );

numbers.stream()
    .filter(
        number -&gt; number &gt; 20
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>25
30</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- MAP -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">map()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>map()</code> operation transforms each stream
                element into another value.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

numbers.stream()
    .map(
        number -&gt; number * number
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>1
4
9
16
25</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- DISTINCT -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">distinct()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>distinct()</code> operation removes duplicate
                elements according to the stream elements' equality.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        10,
        20,
        10,
        30,
        20
    );

numbers.stream()
    .distinct()
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>10
20
30</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- SORTED -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">sorted()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>sorted()</code> operation returns elements in sorted
                order.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        50,
        10,
        40,
        20,
        30
    );

numbers.stream()
    .sorted()
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>10
20
30
40
50</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- CUSTOM SORT -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Sorting with Comparator
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of(
        "Amit",
        "Neha",
        "Rahul",
        "Priya"
    );

names.stream()
    .sorted(
        (a, b) -&gt;
            b.compareTo(a)
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Rahul
Priya
Neha
Amit</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- LIMIT -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">limit()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>limit()</code> operation restricts the stream to a
                maximum number of elements.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        10,
        20,
        30,
        40,
        50
    );

numbers.stream()
    .limit(3)
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>10
20
30</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- SKIP -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">skip()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>skip()</code> operation discards the first specified
                number of elements.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>numbers.stream()
    .skip(2)
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- FLATMAP -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">flatMap()</h3>

            <p>
                <code>flatMap()</code> is useful when each element produces
                another collection or stream and you want one flattened
                stream.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; groups =
    List.of(
        List.of("A", "B"),
        List.of("C", "D")
    );

groups.stream()
    .flatMap(
        group -&gt; group.stream()
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>A
B
C
D</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- REDUCE -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">reduce()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>reduce()</code> operation combines stream elements
                into a single result.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(10, 20, 30, 40);

int sum =
    numbers.stream()
        .reduce(
            0,
            (a, b) -&gt; a + b
        );

System.out.println(sum);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>100</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- COUNT -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">count()</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

<pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(10, 20, 30, 40);
    long count =
    numbers.stream()
        .filter(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 20
        )
        .count();

System.out.println(count);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>2</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- MIN MAX -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                min() and max()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Optional&lt;Integer&gt; minimum =
    numbers.stream()
        .min(Integer::compareTo);

Optional&lt;Integer&gt; maximum =
    numbers.stream()
        .max(Integer::compareTo);</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- MATCHING -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Matching Operations
            </h3>

            <p>
                Streams provide matching operations for checking conditions
                across elements.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>boolean any =
    numbers.stream()
        .anyMatch(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 30
        );

boolean all =
    numbers.stream()
        .allMatch(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 0
        );

boolean none =
    numbers.stream()
        .noneMatch(
            number -&gt; number &lt; 0
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="table-responsive mt-3">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Operation</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>anyMatch()</code></td>
                            <td>At least one element matches.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>allMatch()</code></td>
                            <td>Every element matches.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>noneMatch()</code></td>
                            <td>No element matches.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- FIND FIRST -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                findFirst() and findAny()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Optional&lt;Integer&gt; first =
    numbers.stream()
        .filter(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 20
        )
        .findFirst();

first.ifPresent(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The result is wrapped in <code>Optional</code> because the
                stream may contain no matching element.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- COLLECT -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">collect()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>collect()</code> operation gathers stream results
                into collections or other result containers.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; evenNumbers =
    numbers.stream()
        .filter(
            number -&gt; number % 2 == 0
        )
        .collect(
            Collectors.toList()
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TO LIST -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Collecting with toList()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; result =
    names.stream()
        .filter(
            name -&gt; name.length() &gt; 4
        )
        .collect(
            Collectors.toList()
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TO SET -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Collecting with toSet()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Set&lt;String&gt; uniqueNames =
    names.stream()
        .collect(
            Collectors.toSet()
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- JOINING -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Collectors.joining()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>joining()</code> can combine strings into a single
                string.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><cod<pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of(
        "Amit",
        "Neha",
        "Rahul",
        "Priya"
    );
 String result =
    names.stream()
        .collect(
            Collectors.joining(", ")
        );

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit, Neha, Rahul</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- GROUPING -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                groupingBy()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>groupingBy()</code> groups elements according to a
                classification function.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;Integer, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; grouped =
    names.stream()
        .collect(
            Collectors.groupingBy(
                String::length
            )
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PARTITIONING -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                partitioningBy()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>partitioningBy()</code> divides elements into two
                groups based on a boolean condition.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Map&lt;Boolean, List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; partitioned =
    numbers.stream()
        .collect(
            Collectors.partitioningBy(
                number -&gt; number % 2 == 0
            )
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PEEK -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                peek()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>peek()</code> can be useful for observing elements while
                debugging a stream pipeline. It should not normally be used
                as the main mechanism for application side effects.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

<pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(10, 20, 30, 40);                    
    numbers.stream()
    .filter(
        number -&gt; number &gt; 10
    )
    .peek(
        number -&gt;
            System.out.println(
                "Before map: " + number
            )
    )
    .map(
        number -&gt; number * 2
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Before map: 20
40
Before map: 30
60
Before map: 40
80</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- LAZY -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Lazy Evaluation
            </h3>

            <p>
                Intermediate operations are lazy. They are not generally
                executed until a terminal operation is invoked.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

<pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(10, 20, 30, 40);   
    Stream&lt;Integer&gt; stream =
    numbers.stream()
        .filter(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 10
        );

System.out.println(
    "Pipeline created"
);

stream.forEach(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Pipeline created
20
30
40</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- ONE TIME -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Streams Cannot Normally Be Reused
            </h3>

            <p>
                A stream is intended for a single pipeline execution. After a
                terminal operation consumes it, attempting to reuse the same
                stream results in an exception.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Stream&lt;String&gt; stream =
    names.stream();

stream.forEach(
    System.out::println
);

// Do not reuse the same stream here.</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PARALLEL STREAM -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Parallel Streams
            </h3>

            <p>
                A parallel stream can process elements using multiple threads.
                It can be useful for suitable CPU-intensive workloads, but it
                should not be assumed to be faster for every task.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>numbers.parallelStream()
    .filter(
        number -&gt; number &gt; 10
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
                Parallel streams require careful consideration of workload,
                ordering, shared state, thread safety, and performance.
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- OPTIONAL -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Stream and Optional
            </h3>

            <p>
                Operations such as <code>findFirst()</code>,
                <code>findAny()</code>, <code>min()</code>, and
                <code>max()</code> commonly return <code>Optional</code>
                because a result may not exist.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Optional&lt;Integer&gt; result =
    numbers.stream()
        .filter(
            number -&gt; number &gt; 100
        )
        .findFirst();

result.ifPresent(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- PRIMITIVE STREAMS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Primitive Streams
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java provides specialized streams for primitive numeric types:
                <code>IntStream</code>, <code>LongStream</code>, and
                <code>DoubleStream</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>IntStream.range(
        1,
        6
    )
    .forEach(
        System.out::println
    );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STATISTICS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Numeric Stream Operations
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>IntSummaryStatistics statistics =
    numbers.stream()
        .mapToInt(
            Integer::intValue
        )
        .summaryStatistics();

System.out.println(
    statistics.getCount()
);

System.out.println(
    statistics.getSum()
);

System.out.println(
    statistics.getAverage()
);

System.out.println(
    statistics.getMin()
);

System.out.println(
    statistics.getMax()
);</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- REAL WORLD -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 👨‍🏫🤓
            </h3>

            <p>
                Suppose an application needs to find active employees whose
                salary is above a particular threshold and return their names.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Employee&gt; employees =
    getEmployees();

List&lt;String&gt; result =
    employees.stream()
        .filter(
            employee -&gt;
                employee.isActive()
        )
        .filter(
            employee -&gt;
                employee.getSalary() &gt; 50000
        )
        .map(
            Employee::getName
        )
        .sorted()
        .collect(
            Collectors.toList()
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- BENEFITS -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Benefits of Stream API
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Provides concise data-processing pipelines.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Works naturally with lambda expressions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Supports filtering, mapping, sorting, grouping, and
                    reduction.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Encourages declarative programming.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Supports sequential and parallel processing models.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Stream API Best Practices
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Keep stream pipelines readable.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid unnecessary stream operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Prefer method references when they improve readability.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid modifying shared mutable state inside stream
                    operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>peek()</code> mainly for debugging or observation.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use parallel streams only after considering workload and
                    thread-safety implications.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERVIEW -->
        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Interview Questions
            </h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="streamQuestions">

                <!-- Q1 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionOne">

                            What is Stream API?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Stream API provides a functional-style approach
                            for processing sequences of data.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q2 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionTwo">

                            What is the difference between intermediate and terminal operations?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Intermediate operations return another stream and
                            are generally lazy. Terminal operations consume
                            the stream and produce a result or side effect.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q3 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionThree">

                            What is lazy evaluation in streams?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Intermediate stream operations are generally not
                            executed until a terminal operation starts
                            consuming the pipeline.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q4 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionFour">

                            What is the difference between map() and filter()?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>filter()</code> selects elements based on a
                            condition, while <code>map()</code> transforms
                            elements into another form.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q5 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionFive">

                            Can a stream be reused?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            A stream is intended for a single pipeline
                            execution. After a terminal operation, the same
                            stream should not be reused.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q6 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionSix">

                            What is reduce()?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionSix"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>reduce()</code> combines stream elements into
                            a single result using an accumulator operation.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q7 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionSeven">

                            What is flatMap()?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionSeven"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>flatMap()</code> transforms each element into
                            a stream and then flattens those streams into one
                            stream.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- Q8 -->
                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#streamQuestionEight">

                            Are parallel streams always faster?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="streamQuestionEight"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#streamQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            No. Performance depends on the workload, data
                            size, operation costs, hardware, ordering
                            requirements, and thread-safety characteristics.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SUMMARY -->
        <section class="mb-4">

            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

                <h5 class="fw-bold">
                    Summary
                </h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    The Java Stream API provides a powerful way to process
                    collections and other data sources using declarative
                    pipelines. Important operations include filter, map,
                    flatMap, distinct, sorted, limit, skip, reduce, collect,
                    groupingBy, matching operations, and primitive streams.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


